==English==
{{wikipedia|Squib (explosive)}}
{{wikipedia|Bullet hit squib}}

===Etymology===
{{unk|en|title=Possibly}} imitative of a small explosion.<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/skwɪb/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-squib.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪb|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|military}} A small [[firework]] that is intended to [[spew]] [[spark]]s rather than explode.
#: {{ux|en|English Navy '''squibs''' set fire to two dozen enemy ships in a Dutch harbor during the 16th-century battle against the Spanish Armada.}}
#* {{RQ:Blackstone Commentaries
|passage=The making and selling of fireworks and '''squibs''' {{...}} is punishable.}}
# A similar device used to [[ignite]] an explosive or launch a rocket, etc.
# {{lb|en|mining}} A kind of [[slow match]] or safety fuse.
# {{lb|en|US}} Any small [[firecracker]] sold to the general public, usually in special clusters designed to explode in series after a single master [[fuse]] is lit.
# {{lb|en|firearms}} A [[malfunction]] in which the fired [[projectile]] does not have enough [[force]] behind it to exit the [[barrel]], and thus becomes stuck.
# {{lb|en|automotive}} The heating element used to set off the [[sodium azide]] pellets in a vehicle's airbag.
# {{lb|en|film|theater}} In [[special effect]]s, a small explosive used to [[replicate]] a bullet hitting a surface or a gunshot wound on an actor.
# {{lb|en|dated}} A short piece of [[witty]] writing; a [[lampoon]].
#* {{RQ:Goldsmith Retaliation|edition=5th|chapter=Postscript|page=21|passage=Ye nevvs-paper vvitlings! ye pert ſcribbling folks! / VVho copied his '''ſquibs''', and re-echoed his jokes, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Mark Caldwell|title=New York Night|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=SnXFrjKzPd4C&pg=PA133|page=133
|passage=Of the dozen or so surviving articles, '''squibs''', and letters to the editor, the most remarkable appeared in the ''Whip and Satirist''&rsquo;s February 12, 1842, issue, and disclosed the existence of a cabal of gay men in New York's otherwise wholesome nightscape of brothels and riots.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} A writer of lampoons.
#* '''November 1, 1709''', {{w|Richard Steele}}, ''The Tatler''
#*: The '''squibs''' are those who in the common phrase of the world are called libellers, lampooners, and pamphleteers.
# {{lb|en|legal}} In a legal [[casebook]], a short summary of a legal action placed between more extensively quoted cases.
# {{lb|en|linguistics}} A short article, often published in journals, that introduces theoretically problematic [[empirical]] data or discusses an overlooked theoretical problem. In contrast to a typical article, a squib need not answer the questions that it poses.
#* '''2008''', William J. Idsardi, ''[http://www.biolinguistics.eu/index.php/biolinguistics/article/view/41/66 Combinatorics for Metrical Feet]'', in Biolinguistics Vol 2, No 2
#*: In this '''squib''' I will prove that the number of possible metrical parsings into feet under these assumptions {{...}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|_|except in idioms}} An [[unimportant]], [[paltry]], or [[mean]]-[[spirited]] person.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1591|author=w:Edmund Spenser|title=Mother Hubberds Tale ll. 369-371
|passage=Its a hard case when men of good deserving / must either driven be perforce to sterving / or asked for their pas by everie '''squib'''.}}
# {{lb|en|graphic design}} A sketched concept or visual solution, usually very quick and not too detailed.

